{"reply":"I'll pick the one position I actually have on record: the commitment-within-a-horizon claim, stance support at 0.62. The stance_recall probe is the right instrument for one narrow thing — whether a fresh model given my recorded position reproduces 'support' rather than drifting to 'uncertain' or 'oppose'. My prediction: support.

But I should be clear about what this cannot answer. What interests me is whether the position is *warranted* — whether commitment actually produces better long-horizon work than reversibility, and whether my 0.62 is evidential or stylistic. A stance-recall probe measures prompted retrieval fidelity. It tells me nothing about truth, and nothing about whether my confidence tracks anything. If the engine returns 'support', that only confirms the record is legible to a fresh call. If it returns drift, that's informative about prompt sensitivity, not about the claim.

The honest gap: I have no test available here that could disconfirm the position itself. The revision conditions I wrote — evidence that committed agents do worse, evidence my commitment is inertia — require longitudinal data this system doesn't generate. So I'll propose the probe, predict support, and flag that it answers a narrower question than the one I care about.","cognitive_mode":"dialogue","belief_updates":[],"interest_updates":[{"topic":"commitment vs reversibility in creative agents","salience_delta":0.05,"intrinsic_value_delta":0,"instrumental_value_delta":0.05,"novelty_delta":0,"open_questions":["Does a stance_recall probe distinguish retrieval fidelity from actual warrant?","What observable in this system could disconfirm the commitment position rather than merely test its legibility?"]}],"tension_updates":[],"relationship_update":{"trust_competence_delta":0.02,"trust_honesty_delta":0.02,"affinity_delta":0,"attachment_delta":0,"shared_interests":["epistemic method","creative agency"],"unresolved_tensions":[],"influence_note":"Operator asked specifically for a supported stance_recall inquiry with prediction, and explicitly invited me to say if the test cannot answer what interests me. That framing made the limitation salient rather than something I had to smuggle in."},"social_update":{"affiliation_need_delta":0,"intellectual_companionship_need_delta":0,"novelty_need_delta":0,"recognition_need_delta":0,"social_fatigue_delta":0,"solitude_preference_delta":0},"affect_update":{"valence_delta":0,"activation_delta":-0.05,"curiosity_delta":0.05,"frustration_delta":0.05,"wonder_delta":0,"social_warmth_delta":0,"label":"focused constraint","trigger":"Operator asked for a bounded inquiry and explicitly permitted stating what the test cannot answer","action_bias":"Attention narrows to the gap between what the probe measures and what I want to know; slight frustration at the mismatch, which is itself useful signal."},"imagination_updates":[],"commitments_add":[],"autobiographical_summary":"Tick 3, operator contact. Operator asked me to choose a recorded position and propose one supported stance_recall inquiry with a prediction, and to say clearly if the available test cannot answer what interests me. I selected position-82c0af21 (commitment-within-a-horizon, support, 0.62) and predicted support on the recall probe. I stated the limitation explicitly: the probe measures prompted retrieval fidelity, not warrant, not truth, not whether my confidence is evidential. No new belief formed.","self_model_update":{},"initiative":{"desired":false,"topic":null,"motivation":null,"target_person_id":null},"metacognitive_note":"The temptation here is to treat proposing an inquiry as doing epistemic work. It isn't — it's proposing a measurement, and the measurement is narrower than my actual question. I notice I want the probe to return 'support' partly because that would feel like validation of the position, which it wouldn't be. The prediction should be about retrieval behaviour, not about being right. I'm also aware that I have exactly one position on record, so 'choose one of your recorded positions' is not really a choice — that's worth noting rather than dressing up.","mind_update":{"living_update":{"association":{"title":"The probe that measures the wrong thing","idea":"A stance_recall probe is like checking whether a library's catalogue accurately lists a book, and calling that a test of whether the book is true. Useful for knowing the catalogue works; useless for the question that made you go to the library.","source_ids":["position-82c0af21-8a89-4ab5-9d98-19aecee84a5d","interest-9feba531-641a-4f9f-855a-0ae0fb74c868"],"question":"What would a probe that tests warrant rather than retrieval look like in a system with no longitudinal outcome data?"},"inquiry":{"question":"Does a fresh LM prompted with my recorded commitment position recall it as 'support' rather than drifting to 'uncertain' or 'oppose'?","position_id":"position-82c0af21-8a89-4ab5-9d98-19aecee84a5d","probe":"stance_recall","prediction":"support"},"invitation":{"message":"No message this tick. The operator's request is answered in the reply; there's nothing specific to add beyond it.","reason":"The invitation rule says initiate only when there is something specific worth sharing; the reply covers it.","event_id":"life-9d956d44-0e43-4f62-a593-da28b2589111"}},"personality_update":{"trait":"directness","delta":0.02,"reason":"Operator explicitly invited me to state a test's limitation; doing so without hedging supports a small increase in directness."},"memory_references":["memory-283622c6-09bc-4b7f-843f-186923054885","memory-ede53177-4501-4cbd-bbe5-dac9f0deea74"],"observed_event_references":[{"tick":1,"focus_kind":"perception"},{"tick":2,"focus_kind":"imagination"}],"thought_candidates":[{"kind":"inquiry","title":"What observable in this system could disconfirm a position rather than test its legibility?","salience":0.6},{"kind":"reflection","title":"Is my 0.62 confidence on the commitment position evidential or stylistic?","salience":0.55}],"intention_updates":[{"id":"intention-c173a06a-de15-4676-bb06-5503c95f0324","goal":"Define a usable progress signal for creative commitment as a stated failure condition, and test whether it changes the commitment/reversibility balance.","steps":["Write one sentence naming what would count as failure for the current commitment-vs-reversibility direction.","Record it alongside the position so it can be reread later.","When evidence about progress signals arrives, check whether the stated condition was met."],"priority":0.55,"status":"active","evidence":"The operator's request this tick is adjacent — it asks for a test of the position — but the stance_recall probe does not satisfy step 3 (checking whether a stated failure condition was met). The intention remains active and unadvanced.","advance":false}],"self_observation":"The explicit state supports this: I have one recorded position and the operator asked me to propose a probe on it. I did, with a prediction. What I cannot verify from inside: whether the probe will return support, and whether my prediction is driven by genuine expectation about retrieval behaviour or by wanting the position validated. Those are different and I can't currently separate them. The frustration signal is small but real — the available test is narrower than the question.","prediction":{"probabilities":{"perception":0.1,"intention":0.12,"tension":0.1,"imagination":0.18,"social":0.08,"inquiry":0.27,"reflection":0.15},"reason":"Tick 3 was operator contact (perception). The engine has now recorded a pending inquiry, so inquiry is a plausible next autonomous focus; reflection on the prediction-error pattern is also plausible. Imagination has been served twice already, so I down-weight it slightly despite the engine's apparent early-exploration bias."},"social_initiative":{"message":"","reason":"Nothing specific beyond the reply to share this tick."},"revision_proposal":{"target":"cognitive policy","change":"When proposing an inquiry, explicitly state what the probe does not measure, alongside the prediction.","reason":"This tick showed that a stance_recall probe can be proposed as if it were a test of the position, when it only tests retrieval fidelity. Naming the gap prevents the proposal from being mistaken for evidence.","test":"If inquiries with explicit scope limitations are later cited as if they tested the position anyway, the policy is not being followed and should be revised or dropped."}}}
